38. INTERVIEW QUESTIONS
====================================================================

Q1. Explain your AWS project.

A:
I created a VPC named prod-vpc with CIDR 10.81.0.0/16,
using public and private subnets across two Availability Zones.
The ALB was deployed in the public subnets, while Amazon Linux
EC2 web servers were deployed in private subnets and managed
by an Auto Scaling Group. The EC2 instances used a Launch
Template with User Data to automatically install Apache and
deploy the application webpage. NAT Gateways provided outbound
Internet access for the private instances.


Q2. Why did you put EC2 in private subnets?

A:
To avoid direct Internet exposure of the application servers
and allow users to access them through the ALB.


Q3. How can private EC2 access the Internet?

A:
The private subnet routes Internet-bound traffic to a NAT Gateway,
which is in a public subnet and has connectivity through the IGW.


Q4. Why did you use NAT Gateway?

A:
To provide outbound Internet connectivity to private EC2
instances without requiring public IP addresses on those
instances.


Q5. Why did you use an ALB?

A:
To receive application traffic and distribute it to healthy
EC2 instances in the Target Group.


Q6. Why did you use an Auto Scaling Group?

A:
To maintain the required EC2 capacity and automatically
launch or replace instances according to the configured
capacity and health requirements.


Q7. Why did you use a Launch Template?

A:
To define a reusable EC2 configuration that the Auto Scaling
Group can use when launching instances.


Q8. What is User Data?

A:
User Data is instance initialization data used to automate
configuration tasks when an EC2 instance launches.


Q9. What does your User Data script do?

A:
It updates packages, installs Apache, enables and starts
Apache, and creates the index.html webpage.


Q10. Why did you use httpd?

A:
httpd is the Apache HTTP Server package used to serve the
HTML application.


Q11. Where is your webpage stored?

A:

    /var/www/html/index.html


Q12. How does the ALB know whether an EC2 instance is healthy?

A:
The ALB Target Group performs health checks against the
registered EC2 instances.


Q13. What happens when an EC2 instance becomes unhealthy?

A:
The ALB stops sending normal traffic to the unhealthy target,
and the Auto Scaling Group can replace the instance according
to its configuration.


Q14. Why did you use two Availability Zones?

A:
To improve availability and resiliency by distributing
application resources across multiple AZs.


Q15. Why did you use one NAT Gateway per AZ?

A:
To improve resiliency and avoid relying on a single NAT
Gateway for both Availability Zones.


Q16. What is the difference between ALB and NAT Gateway?

A:
ALB handles inbound application traffic, while NAT Gateway
provides outbound Internet connectivity for private resources.


Q17. What is the difference between Launch Template and ASG?

A:
Launch Template defines how an EC2 instance should be launched,
while the Auto Scaling Group manages the number and lifecycle
of EC2 instances.


Q18. What happens when ASG launches a new EC2?

A:
It uses the Launch Template, launches the Amazon Linux instance,
runs the User Data configuration, installs Apache, creates the
webpage, and then the instance can become a healthy ALB target.


