    - Lower latency
    - Better scalability


====================================================================
19. CACHE MISS
====================================================================

Cache Miss means:

    Requested content is not currently available
    as a valid cached object for that request.


Flow:

    User
      |
      v
    CloudFront
      |
      | Cache Miss
      v
    Origin
      |
      v
    ALB
      |
      v
    EC2


Then:

    Origin response
        |
        v
    CloudFront
        |
        +---- Return to user
        |
        +---- Cache object


====================================================================
20. CACHE HIT RATIO
====================================================================

Cache Hit Ratio tells us how many requests are
served from cache compared with total requests.


Concept:

    Cache Hit Ratio
        =
    Cache Hits / Total Requests


Example:

    Total requests:
        1000

    Cache hits:
        800


    Cache Hit Ratio:
        80%


Higher cache hit ratio generally means more requests
are served from cache rather than reaching the origin.


====================================================================
21. WHAT CAN BE CACHED?
====================================================================

Good candidates:

    - CSS
    - JavaScript
    - Images
    - Fonts
    - Videos
    - Static HTML
    - Public API responses where appropriate


Examples:

    /css/style.css
    /js/app.js
    /images/logo.png
    /images/product.jpg


Be careful with:

    - Personalized pages
    - Login pages
    - User-specific content
    - Shopping carts
    - Dynamic authenticated API responses
    - Frequently changing data


====================================================================
22. CACHE KEY
====================================================================

CloudFront uses a cache key to determine whether
a requested object matches an existing cached object.


Cache key can include:

    - URL path
    - Query strings
    - Headers
    - Cookies
    - Other configured values


Example:

    /product?id=100


and:

    /product?id=200


If the query string is included in the cache key,
these can be treated as different cached objects.


AWS cache policies control which headers, cookies and
query strings are included in the cache key. :contentReference[oaicite:9]{index=9}


====================================================================
23. CACHE POLICY
====================================================================

Cache Policy controls:

    - Cache key
    - TTL
    - Headers included in cache key
    - Cookies included in cache key
    - Query strings included in cache key
    - Compression settings


A cache policy is attached to a cache behavior.


AWS recommends using cache policies for current CloudFront
cache configuration. :contentReference[oaicite:10]{index=10}


====================================================================
24. CACHE POLICY - SIMPLE EXAMPLE
====================================================================

Suppose:

    URL:

    /images/logo.png


We don't need:

    Cookies
    Query strings
    Authorization headers


A simple cache policy can keep the cache key small.

Result:

    Many users
        |
        v
    Same cache object
        |
        v
    /images/logo.png


This increases cache reuse.


====================================================================
25. ORIGIN REQUEST POLICY
====================================================================

Origin Request Policy controls what CloudFront sends
to the origin.


It can include:

    - Headers
    - Cookies
    - Query strings


Important difference:

    Cache Policy
        =
    What identifies a cached object?


    Origin Request Policy
        =
    What should be sent to the origin?


AWS specifically separates these two concepts so you can
send information to the origin without necessarily making
that information part of the cache key. :contentReference[oaicite:11]{index=11}


====================================================================
26. CACHE POLICY VS ORIGIN REQUEST POLICY
====================================================================

CACHE POLICY:

    Controls cache key.

    Example:

        Query string:
            language

        Cookie:
            none

        Header:
            none


ORIGIN REQUEST POLICY:

    Controls what is forwarded to the origin.


Easy memory:

    CACHE POLICY
        =
    CACHE DECISION


    ORIGIN REQUEST POLICY
        =
    ORIGIN REQUEST


====================================================================
27. TTL
====================================================================

TTL means:

    Time To Live


TTL controls how long CloudFront considers an
object valid in its cache before it needs to
check the origin again.


CloudFront cache policies use:

    Minimum TTL
    Default TTL
    Maximum TTL


AWS documents these three TTL values as part of
cache policy configuration. :contentReference[oaicite:12]{index=12}


====================================================================
28. MINIMUM TTL
====================================================================

Minimum TTL defines the minimum amount of time
CloudFront keeps an object in cache before checking
the origin again.


Example:

    Minimum TTL:
        60 seconds


The object is not normally revalidated before the
minimum TTL has elapsed.


IMPORTANT:

If Minimum TTL is greater than 0, CloudFront can cache
content for at least that duration even when origin
headers contain no-cache/no-store/private directives.


AWS explicitly warns about this behavior. :contentReference[oaicite:13]{index=13}


====================================================================
29. DEFAULT TTL
====================================================================

Default TTL is used when the origin does not provide
Cache-Control or Expires headers.


Example:

    Default TTL:
        86400 seconds


86400 seconds:

    24 hours


====================================================================
30. MAXIMUM TTL
====================================================================

Maximum TTL defines the maximum amount of time an object
can remain cached under the cache policy.


Example:

    Maximum TTL:
        31536000


Approximately:

    1 year


The actual values should be selected based on
application requirements.


====================================================================
31. CACHE-CONTROL HEADER
====================================================================

The origin can return:

    Cache-Control


Example:

    Cache-Control: max-age=3600


This tells caching systems how long the response
can be considered fresh.


CloudFront uses origin Cache-Control/Expires headers
together with the cache policy TTL settings. :contentReference[oaicite:14]{index=14}


====================================================================
32. EXAMPLE OF CACHING
====================================================================

Suppose:

    /images/logo.png


Cache policy:

    Minimum TTL:
        0

    Default TTL:
        86400

    Maximum TTL:
        31536000


If the origin does not specify Cache-Control:

    CloudFront can use:

        Default TTL = 24 hours


The image can be served from cache during that period,
subject to the cache policy and other CloudFront behavior.


