63. CLOUDFRONT INVALIDATION
====================================================================

Suppose we have:

    /index.html


CloudFront cached an old version.

You update:

    index.html


But users still receive the old cached version.


You can create an:

    INVALIDATION


Example:

    /index.html


or:

    /*


Invalidation tells CloudFront to remove specified
objects from edge caches so subsequent requests
retrieve fresh content.


====================================================================
64. INVALIDATION EXAMPLE
====================================================================

Old:

    Welcome to Mind Circuit Facebook page


New:

    Welcome to Mind Circuit CloudFront page


CloudFront may still have:

    old index.html


Create invalidation:

    /index.html


Then:

    New request
        |
        v
    CloudFront
        |
        v
    Origin
        |
        v
    New index.html


IMPORTANT:

Invalidation is different from TTL expiration.


TTL:

    Object naturally becomes stale/expired
    according to caching rules.


Invalidation:

    You explicitly request removal of cached objects.


====================================================================
65. INVALIDATION COMMAND CONCEPT
====================================================================

AWS CLI example:

aws cloudfront create-invalidation \
    --distribution-id DISTRIBUTION_ID \
    --paths "/index.html"


For all objects:

aws cloudfront create-invalidation \
    --distribution-id DISTRIBUTION_ID \
    --paths "/*"


Use "/*" carefully because it invalidates a large
set of cached objects.


