86. 60-SECOND INTERVIEW ANSWER
====================================================================

"I used Amazon CloudFront as a CDN in front of my
Application Load Balancer. CloudFront receives user
requests at edge locations and serves cacheable content
from the edge when available. If the content is not
cached, CloudFront forwards the request to the ALB,
which sends it to the healthy EC2 instances in the
private subnets.

I configure the ALB as the CloudFront origin and define
cache behaviors, cache policies, origin request policies,
TTL values, viewer protocol policy, allowed HTTP methods
and HTTPS settings. For a custom domain, I use an ACM
certificate in us-east-1. I can also integrate AWS WAF
and CloudFront logging.

For security, I restrict the ALB so that the origin is
not unnecessarily exposed directly to the Internet and
can use the AWS-managed CloudFront origin-facing prefix
list where appropriate."


